# ============================================================================
# What may enter the Docker build context.
#
# Read the FIRST rule as the important one; the rest is size.
# ============================================================================

# ── 1. THE KEY. Never, under any circumstance. ──────────────────────────────
# config.js holds an ElevenLabs API key (and optionally an Anthropic one), and
# build.js INLINES IT VERBATIM into dist/index.html unless it is passed
# --no-config. So a config.js that reaches the build context does not merely sit
# in a discarded builder stage — it is baked, in cleartext, into the one file
# this image exists to serve, and anyone who loads the page can read it out of
# view-source. It is gitignored for the same reason; .dockerignore is the same
# fence for the other copy mechanism.
#
# This is belt. The braces are in the Dockerfile: the builder stage hard-fails
# if config.js is present anyway, and greps the built artifact for a key prefix.
# Two independent guards, because one typo here would be silent.
config.js

# ── 1b. config.public.js IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED HERE ────────────────────
# Stated as a rule rather than left as an omission, because the next person to
# read rule 1 will reasonably wonder why one config file is fenced out and the
# other is not, and "they both look like config" is a good enough reason to
# "fix" it. Adding it would break the image build outright — see the Dockerfile
# COPY allowlist, which names it.
#
# They are opposite files. config.js HOLDS a credential; config.public.js holds
# two relative endpoints (/api/tts, /api/llm) and the statement that there is no
# credential in this page — ask the server instead. It is tracked in git for the
# same reason, and it has to reach the builder or `--public-config` has nothing
# to inline.
#
# Which means every fence in rule 1 is structurally unavailable for it: it
# cannot be gitignored, cannot be kept out of the context, and cannot be left
# off the allowlist. Its guard is therefore a CONTENT check instead of a
# location one — build.js refuses to build if the file ever contains anything
# key-shaped or any populated credential field. If you are about to put a key
# in it "just to test something", that is the check you will hit, and it is
# right.

# ── 1c. .env — the proxy's secrets, and why they are not excluded here ──────
# Also deliberately absent, and for the opposite reason to 1b.
#
# The aib-proxy keeps the ElevenLabs and Anthropic keys in a .env. Excluding
# `.env` here would hide it from the context — and from the Dockerfile's
# context-audit stage, whose whole job is to fail when a secret is sitting in
# the context. The guard would still technically fire on a regression of this
# line, but the everyday signal — "your proxy's keys are in the deck's build
# context, move them" — would be gone, and that signal is the useful one.
#
# So the .env check is left able to fire, and the correct place for the proxy's
# env file is OUTSIDE this repo entirely: /srv/aib-proxy/aib-proxy.env, passed
# at run time with --env-file. docs/DEPLOY-PROXY.md is the procedure. If the
# audit stage ever fails on a .env, do not add a line here — move the file.
#
# proxy/ itself is not excluded either, for the same reason: exclude the
# directory and nothing under it can ever reach /ctx, which would make the .env
# check incapable of failing. The Dockerfile says at length why a guard that
# cannot fail is worse than no guard; this is that rule applied.

# ── 2. dist/ — deliberately excluded, and this is load-bearing ──────────────
# dist/index.html IS committed in this repo (it is the thing you double-click).
# Keeping it out of the context means the image PHYSICALLY CANNOT be assembled
# from a pre-built host artifact: the builder stage has to run `node build.js`
# and generate it, or COPY --from finds nothing and the build fails. An image
# that can quietly ship whatever happened to be sitting in dist/ on the
# packager's laptop is not reproducible from source, it just looks like it is.
dist/

# ── 3. Bulk this build does not read ────────────────────────────────────────
# The canvas target (`node build.js`, no flags) reads exactly: index.html,
# assets/fonts.css, and src/*.js|css. It never opens vendor/ or the GLB — those
# belong to `--3d`, which is explicitly NOT what this image serves. Excluding
# them keeps ~8 MB out of every context upload.
#
# If this image is ever repointed at the 3D build, these three lines are what
# you delete first — the build would otherwise succeed and produce a 3D page
# with no avatar in it, which presents as the canvas bust rather than an error.
vendor/
assets/*.glb
assets/avatar-glb.js

# ── 4. Ordinary noise ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
node_modules/
package-lock.json
shots/
tests/
tools/
docs/
shoot.js
README.md
PR-DRAFT.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
*.md
